{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take positive $\dot Q$ to mean net removal of thermal <energy> per unit mass. The <isobaric thermal stability of a heated ideal gas> depends on the density change accompanying a temperature disturbance. For an <ideal gas> of fixed composition, $p=\rho k_BT/\bar m$, so constant <pressure> gives $\rho T=\text{constant}$. Therefore the net cooling can be written
$$
\mathcal L(T)=CT^{\alpha-1}-H,
$$
where $C>0$ is constant for this pressure. At thermal equilibrium $T=T_0$, $CT_0^{\alpha-1}=H$.

The <first law of thermodynamics> at constant pressure includes the expansion work, so the relevant heat capacity is the <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>, $c_p$, rather than the constant-volume value. Linearizing its temperature equation gives
$$
c_p\frac{d\,\delta T}{dt}
=-\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial T}\right)_p\delta T
=-\frac{(\alpha-1)H}{T_0}\delta T.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\delta T\propto e^{st},\qquad
s=\frac{(1-\alpha)H}{c_pT_0}.}
$$
For optically thin <Bremsstrahlung>, $\alpha=1/2$, so
$$
\boxed{s=\frac{H}{2c_pT_0}>0:\quad\text{the equilibrium is thermally unstable}.}
$$
A hotter parcel expands and becomes less dense. Its cooling per unit mass, proportional to $T^{-1/2}$ at fixed pressure, decreases while its <cosmic ray> heating stays fixed, making it hotter still. A colder, denser parcel cools faster and runs away in the opposite direction. Here $H$ has units $\mathrm{W\,kg^{-1}}$ and $c_pT_0$ units $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}$, so $s$ is an inverse time. The conclusion assumes the disturbance remains close to pressure equilibrium and neglects <heat conduction> and changes of composition. The relevant $\alpha$ is the temperature exponent in the supplied cooling law.

= b
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the nonrelativistic moving-conductor Ohm law in the form
$$
\mathbf E=\frac{\mathbf j}{\sigma}-\mathbf u\times\mathbf B.
$$
The <Maxwell equations> give <Faraday's law of induction>, $\partial_t\mathbf B=-\nabla\times\mathbf E$. For a nonmagnetic conductor with constant permeability $\mu_0$, the quasistatic <Ampere-Maxwell law> gives $\mathbf j\simeq(\nabla\times\mathbf B)/\mu_0$. This step neglects <displacement current>, requiring it to be small compared with conduction current; nonrelativistic velocity alone is not the complete justification.

For spatially uniform scalar <electrical conductivity> $\sigma$,
$$
\partial_t\mathbf B
=\nabla\times(\mathbf u\times\mathbf B)
-\frac{1}{\mu_0\sigma}\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf B).
$$
Apply the identity $\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf B)=\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf B)-\nabla^2\mathbf B$ and <Gauss's law for magnetism>, $\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$. The <resistive induction equation> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t}
\simeq\nabla\times(\mathbf u\times\mathbf B)
+\eta\nabla^2\mathbf B,\qquad
\eta=\frac1{\mu_0\sigma}.}
$$
The first term transports and deforms the <magnetic field> with the moving fluid; the second diffuses it. The <magnetic diffusivity> $\eta$ has units $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$, so both terms have units tesla per second. No assumption of <incompressible flow> was needed. If $\sigma$ varies spatially, the <conductivity-gradient term in magnetic induction>, $-\nabla\eta\times(\nabla\times\mathbf B)$, must also be retained.

= c
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a plane-parallel slab, all equilibrium quantities depend only on height $z$. The vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> equation and the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> are
$$
\frac{dp}{dz}=-\rho\frac{d\psi}{dz},\qquad
\frac{d^2\psi}{dz^2}=4\pi G\rho.
$$
With the <polytropic equation of state> $p=K\rho^2$ and constant $K$,
$$
2K\rho\frac{d\rho}{dz}=-\rho\frac{d\psi}{dz}.
$$
In the gas interior, where $\rho>0$, divide by $\rho$ and differentiate once:
$$
2K\frac{d^2\rho}{dz^2}=-\frac{d^2\psi}{dz^2}=-4\pi G\rho.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\frac{d^2\rho}{dz^2}+\frac{2\pi G}{K}\rho=0.}
$$
The coefficient $2\pi G/K$ has units inverse square metres. The <index-one plane-parallel polytrope> illustrates the resulting structure: symmetry about $z=0$ gives $\rho(z)=\rho_c\cos(kz)$, with $k=\sqrt{2\pi G/K}$. A slab bounded by vacuum ends at its first zero, $|z|=\pi/(2k)$; the negative continuation of the cosine is not physical <mass density>.

